        {
          "paragraph": 3,
          "status": "verbatim-korean-new-english-translation",
          "sourceExcerpt": "text-classification-and-counts",
          "sourcePages": "424",
          "english": "A key digital-humanities method for investigating these questions is text classification. By statistically identifying linguistic similarities and differences across a large corpus, it enables computers to handle hundreds or thousands of texts that would be difficult for a human researcher to read and compare directly. Early text classification began with bag-of-words models based on word frequency. This method quantifies documents by counting how often each word appears without considering context, and estimates genre tendencies through patterns in which particular words frequently occur together. For example, if king, madness and daughter occur frequently in King Lear, comparison with similar vocabulary distributions in other tragedies can support inferences about statistical features of tragic language. This simple frequency-based approach is limited, however, in its ability to reflect deeper linguistic dimensions such as sentence structure, semantic context and shifts in tone.",
          "koreanSHA256": "6293c7fb0402cabc5de02e5972868a7be9302d932beb576d681d25f5b819ddbe"
        }
      ]
    },
